## 5. Indexing and the grounding discipline

**Two jobs, never confused.** The **code graph** is the authority for what exists and its exact signature; the
**docs lane** explains how a thing is meant to be used. Order on any unfamiliar surface: **docs to understand, code
to confirm, then write.** A method name seen in prose is never confirmation.

| Lane | Authority for |
|---|---|
| `engine` | the installed `rag_wright` package — every `rag_wright.api` / `rag_wright.pack_sdk` call grounds here first |
| `engine-docs` | the engine repo's `docs/` at the installed version's tag + the linked engine skills — how a feature is meant to be wired |
| `project` | this product's own code + libraries |

(Ground against **both** the graph **and** the installed `inspect.signature` — a clone at latest can drift from the
pinned install.) `graphify-out/` is gitignored and local-only; it will not work in CI without a build step.

The engine's `docs/` are NOT shipped in the wheel — index them as the `engine-docs` lane (from the engine repo,
`{{ENGINE_PATH}}/docs/`, at the installed version's tag) and read them there or on GitHub: concepts, architecture, installation, configuration, quickstart, the domain-adaptation guide, reference
pack, and the generated API reference.

**Onboarding a new library:** `uv add` it, then index it by the route that fits (pip-stable → add to the refresh
script's `PKGS`; fast-moving/monorepo → clone-stage at the installed tag; no source + no docs MCP → LLM-extract its
vendor docs), then refresh and query before writing the call. **Gate activation on real configuration, never on
mere importability** (a transitive dep can flip importability-gated behaviour).

### Keeping the engine current

Engine fixes land as engine PRs, but the engine **releases in batches** (release-please; the engine's
`docs/releasing.md`). How this product depends on the engine follows its stage; both modes keep
`rag-wright>={{ENGINE_FLOOR}}` in `pyproject` (a floor, never `==`):

| | **Local co-development** | **Released product** |
|---|---|---|
| when | engine and product developed together on one machine | the product has CI, deploys or other developers |
| dependency | the floor + an editable `[tool.uv.sources]` path to `{{ENGINE_PATH}}` (committed) | the floor from PyPI; `uv.lock` pins the exact version |
| engine changes | picked up immediately: no build, publish or reinstall | adopted per release via Dependabot PRs gated by CI (incl. the LIVE engine-seam tests) |
| release check | `uv sync --no-sources` (CI or before a deploy) installs the published engine; never with `--locked`, and never commit the `uv.lock` it rewrites | CI runs `uv sync --locked` as usual |
| an unreleased engine fix | already visible through the path | a temporary `[tool.uv.sources]` git pin: `rag-wright = { git = "https://github.com/fzaidi-dai/RAG_Wright", rev = "<sha>" }`, then `uv lock`; removed in the Dependabot PR that adopts the release |

Why the release check runs without `--locked`: with the path source, `uv.lock` records the local path, and
`uv sync --no-sources` re-resolves from PyPI (rewriting the lock in that checkout). Switch to the released-product mode
when the product gets CI, deploys or other developers: drop the path source, `uv lock`, commit, and add Dependabot. In
the co-development mode a breaking engine change breaks the product at once; that is useful feedback when deliberate,
so the engine runs the product's suite against its working tree before changing a public symbol or store method.
- Engine bugs found here are fixed **in the engine repo** (a PR with a conventional `fix:` title), never patched
  around in this repo.

